From: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 16:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] UsbCdcNcm: deliver one Ethernet frame per NTB datagram

UsbEthNcmReceive summed the lengths of every datagram in a received NTB
and, if the total came to less than one Ethernet frame, copied them all
into the caller's buffer back to back:

  if (TotalLength < USB_ETHERNET_FRAME_SIZE) {
    for (Index = 0; Index < UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram; Index++) {
      CopyMem ((UINT8 *)Packet + TotalLength, ..., Datagram->DatagramLength);
      TotalLength += Datagram->DatagramLength;
    }
    *PacketLength             = TotalLength;
    UsbEthDriver->NowDatagram = UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram;
  }

Each datagram in an NDP16 table is a distinct Ethernet frame; NCM
aggregation exists so that several frames can share one bulk transfer,
not so that they can be spliced into one. Two 60-byte TCP segments
arriving in a single NTB were handed up as one 120-byte frame: the
second segment vanished and the first was reported over-long. The
caller in NetworkCommon/PxeFunction.c takes *PacketLength at face value
and passes the result straight to SNP, so there is no layer above that
can recover the lost frame.

The threshold made the fault selective in the worst possible way. Bulk
traffic, whose datagrams sum past 0x5F2, took the correct one-frame-at-
a-time path; only small back-to-back packets were aggregated and
corrupted. That is exactly the traffic a TCP connection produces when it
starts losing segments, so the fault sustained itself: dropped segments
provoked retransmissions and duplicate ACKs, those are small packets,
and the gadget batched them into NTBs that triggered the bug again.

On a Raspberry Pi 5 talking Redfish to a BMC over a CDC-NCM gadget the
connection wedged within seconds of coming up, and TcpDxe settled into

  TcpInput: sequence acceptance test failed for segment of TCB 3A60DC18
  TcpInput: Discard a packet

repeating without end. TcpInput answers every unacceptable segment with
an ACK and does not rate-limit them (RFC 1122 4.2.2.20), so once the
receive window had stalled both ends stayed busy indefinitely. Because
the DWC2 host controller polls its deferred endpoints from a TPL_NOTIFY
timer, a link that is never idle also starves everything below
TPL_NOTIFY, and BDS slowed to a crawl.

Return the first datagram and let the existing continuation path walk
the remainder out of the buffer on subsequent calls, which is what that
path was already written to do.

While here, validate the NDP16 table before deriving a datagram count
from it. Length is a UINT16 read off the wire and the count is computed
as (Length - 8) / 4 - 1, which underflows to 255 when Length is 8; the
driver would then walk 255 datagrams' worth of whatever followed. A
table that describes any datagrams at all is at least 12 bytes and a
whole number of words. Handle a well-formed but empty table too: the
count reaches zero for Length 12, and incrementing NowDatagram past
TotalDatagram leaves the two counters permanently unequal, stranding
the driver in the continuation path against a buffer holding nothing.
Both cases now report EFI_NOT_READY, which PxeUndiReceive already maps
to PXE_STATCODE_NO_DATA.

Signed-off-by: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
--- a/MdeModulePkg/Bus/Usb/UsbNetwork/UsbCdcNcm/UsbNcmFunction.c
+++ b/MdeModulePkg/Bus/Usb/UsbNetwork/UsbCdcNcm/UsbNcmFunction.c
@@ -225,15 +225,12 @@
   USB_ETHERNET_DRIVER          *UsbEthDriver;
   EFI_USB_IO_PROTOCOL          *UsbIo;
   UINT32                       TransStatus;
-  UINT8                        Index;
   UINTN                        BulkDataLength;
-  UINTN                        TotalLength;
   USB_NCM_TRANSFER_HEADER_16   *Nth;
   USB_NCM_DATAGRAM_POINTER_16  *Ndp;
   USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM            *Datagram;
 
   UsbEthDriver = USB_ETHERNET_DEV_FROM_THIS (This);
-  TotalLength  = 0;
 
   if (UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram == UsbEthDriver->NowDatagram) {
     Status = gBS->HandleProtocol (
@@ -266,35 +263,53 @@
       return Status;
     }
 
-    Nth                         = (USB_NCM_TRANSFER_HEADER_16 *)UsbEthDriver->BulkBuffer;
-    Ndp                         = (USB_NCM_DATAGRAM_POINTER_16 *)((UINT8 *)UsbEthDriver->BulkBuffer + Nth->NdpIndex);
-    Datagram                    = (USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM *)((UINT8 *)Ndp + sizeof (USB_NCM_DATAGRAM_POINTER_16));
+    Nth      = (USB_NCM_TRANSFER_HEADER_16 *)UsbEthDriver->BulkBuffer;
+    Ndp      = (USB_NCM_DATAGRAM_POINTER_16 *)((UINT8 *)UsbEthDriver->BulkBuffer + Nth->NdpIndex);
+    Datagram = (USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM *)((UINT8 *)Ndp + sizeof (USB_NCM_DATAGRAM_POINTER_16));
+
+    //
+    // An NDP16 table is an 8-byte header, one 4-byte entry per datagram and
+    // a 4-byte null terminator, so a table that carries anything at all is
+    // at least 12 bytes long and a whole number of words. Check that before
+    // deriving a count from it: the expression below underflows to 255 when
+    // Length is 8, and every later call would then walk this buffer as if
+    // it held 255 frames.
+    //
+    if ((Ndp->Length < 12) || ((Ndp->Length % 4) != 0)) {
+      UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram = 0;
+      UsbEthDriver->NowDatagram   = 0;
+      return EFI_NOT_READY;
+    }
+
     UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram = (UINT8)((Ndp->Length - 8) / 4 - 1);
 
-    for (Index = 0; Index < UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram; Index++) {
-      TotalLength += Datagram->DatagramLength;
-      Datagram     = (USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM *)((UINT8 *)Datagram + sizeof (USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM));
+    //
+    // A well-formed NTB may still carry no datagrams. Report no data rather
+    // than advancing NowDatagram past TotalDatagram, which would leave the
+    // two counters permanently unequal and strand the driver in the
+    // continuation path below, re-reading a buffer that holds nothing.
+    //
+    if (UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram == 0) {
+      UsbEthDriver->NowDatagram = 0;
+      return EFI_NOT_READY;
     }
 
-    if (TotalLength < USB_ETHERNET_FRAME_SIZE) {
-      Datagram = (USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM *)((UINT8 *)Ndp + sizeof (USB_NCM_DATAGRAM_POINTER_16));
+    //
+    // One NTB can carry several datagrams and each one is a separate
+    // Ethernet frame, so deliver only the first here; the continuation path
+    // below walks the rest out of the same buffer on subsequent calls.
+    // This driver used to concatenate them into the caller's buffer
+    // whenever they summed to less than one frame, which yields a single
+    // oversized frame in which every datagram after the first is lost. On a
+    // link that mostly carries small packets -- a Redfish host interface
+    // over CDC-NCM, say -- that discards TCP segments about as fast as the
+    // peer retransmits them, stalling the receive window and leaving both
+    // ends in an ACK storm.
+    //
+    UsbEthDriver->NowDatagram++;
 
-      TotalLength = 0;
-      for (Index = 0; Index < UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram; Index++) {
-        CopyMem ((UINT8 *)Packet + TotalLength, (UINT8 *)UsbEthDriver->BulkBuffer + Datagram->DatagramIndex, Datagram->DatagramLength);
-        TotalLength += Datagram->DatagramLength;
-        Datagram     = (USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM *)((UINT8 *)Datagram + sizeof (USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM));
-      }
-
-      *PacketLength             = TotalLength;
-      UsbEthDriver->NowDatagram = UsbEthDriver->TotalDatagram;
-    } else {
-      UsbEthDriver->NowDatagram++;
-
-      Datagram = (USB_NCM_DATA_GRAM *)((UINT8 *)Ndp + sizeof (USB_NCM_DATAGRAM_POINTER_16));
-      CopyMem (Packet, (UINT8 *)UsbEthDriver->BulkBuffer + Datagram->DatagramIndex, Datagram->DatagramLength);
-      *PacketLength = Datagram->DatagramLength;
-    }
+    CopyMem (Packet, (UINT8 *)UsbEthDriver->BulkBuffer + Datagram->DatagramIndex, Datagram->DatagramLength);
+    *PacketLength = Datagram->DatagramLength;
 
     return Status;
   } else {
